<article class="env-remark" id="0193">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0193">Remark <span data-tag="0193">3.1.7.8</span>.</a> Let $\{  f_ i: X_ i \rightarrow Y_ i \} _{i \in I}$ be a collection of homotopy equivalences of simplicial sets indexed by a set $I$, and let $f: \prod _{i \in I} X_ i \rightarrow \prod _{i \in I} Y_ i$ be their product. Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
If $I$ is finite, then $f$ is a homotopy equivalence. This follows from Remark <a data-tag="00U5" href="/tag/00U5">3.1.7.6</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="00GX" href="/tag/00GX">1.2.1.27</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
If each of the simplicial sets $X_{i}$ and $Y_{i}$ is a Kan complex, then $f$ is a homotopy equivalence. This follows from Remark <a data-tag="00U5" href="/tag/00U5">3.1.7.6</a> and Corollary <a data-tag="00HD" href="/tag/00HD">1.2.5.11</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The morphism $f$ need not be a homotopy equivalence in general (see Warning <a data-tag="00GY" href="/tag/00GY">1.2.1.28</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
